    <div class="measure">
      <p>The point is not that simulation replaces calibration. The point is that a carefully built model, with honest loss accounting and its own uncertainty, can be a credible reference and a strong cross-check on the measurement.</p>

      <h2>The 2025 follow-up</h2>
      <p>The work continued at the 2025 AMTA Symposium with "Modeling of Standard Gain Horns Using Advanced Electromagnetic Simulation Techniques" (AMTA Symposium, 2025), where I am a co-author. That paper modeled the same MVG SGH820 X-band (8–12 GHz) horn, with and without its waveguide adapter, using two full-wave solvers in Altair Feko: the Method of Moments (MoM) and the Multilevel Fast Multipole Method (MLFMM). The two solvers agreed within ±0.008 dB on boresight gain and directivity, and MLFMM cut computation time significantly with no loss of accuracy.</p>
      <p>Paper (IEEE Xplore): <a href="https://doi.org/10.23919/AMTA66658.2025.11317872" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://doi.org/10.23919/AMTA66658.2025.11317872</a></p>

      <h2>How to make your sim match your range</h2>
      <p>Most sim versus measurement arguments I get pulled into come down to the same few items. Before you blame the solver, work through this list:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>The models used nominal dimensions, not measured ones.</strong> At the hundredth-of-a-dB level, small flare and aperture differences show up. Breinbjerg et al. (AMTA 2017) showed that an absorber collar increased gain by 0.07 dB (0.09 dB higher directivity and 0.02 dB lower radiation efficiency).</li>
        <li><strong>Account for every loss in the path.</strong> Connectors, adapters, and waveguide transitions all cost something. The supplier could not provide the SMA connector's dimensions, so connector loss was accounted for from a VNA measurement of the connector.</li>
        <li><strong>Compare the same quantity.</strong> Make sure you are comparing gain to gain, at the same reference plane, with the same definition (realized gain versus gain, for example).</li>
        <li><strong>Converge on what you report.</strong> Check mesh convergence on gain itself, not just on S-parameters.</li>
        <li><strong>Put error bars on the simulation.</strong> Run a tolerance study. If the model has no uncertainty, you cannot say whether it "agrees" with anything.</li>
        <li><strong>Know your range's uncertainty budget.</strong> Find the biggest term. If it is the reference horn's gain, a better model may be the cheapest improvement you can make.</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-check with a second solver when the stakes are high.</strong> If two methods agree and measurement does not, look hard at the setup.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Takeaway for engineering managers</h2>
      <p>When simulation and test data disagree, the instinct is often to buy another tool or schedule another test. In my experience the gap usually lives in the model, the loss accounting, or the measurement setup, not in the solver. Budget engineering time for loss characterization and simulation uncertainty, not just for software licenses. A model with honest error bars tells you when you can trust it. A model without them only tells you a number.</p>
    </div>

